  "notes": "Kanye/G.O.O.D.-orbit mainstream trap-rap, and the entry's distinguishing fact is that his beats come from **sample-based** producers as often as synth-trap ones. That shows in three places: real 4-bar progressions and modal shifts sit alongside the vamps (families reach ii–v–i and i–iv–VII–III, `borrowed.V` is 0.15), the swing is loosened to 0.54 with `quantizeStrength` 0.88 for the chopped breaks, and `melody.timbreHint` is `chopped_vocal` for the pitched-up soul samples. The punchline cadences with stop-time gaps are `melody.emptyBarBias` 0.16 — a bar the lead simply does not play, which is what makes the next one land. Verses are 16–24 bars, longer than the lane's norm, and outro beat-switches push `switchUpProb` to 0.3. ⚠ `confidence` medium (research: high-medium). The INFERRED parameter is `session.swing.amount` 0.54: \"looser swing\" on the sample-based cuts is a direction, not a figure, and 0.54 is this author's reading of a loosened-but-not-shuffled 16th grid. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
